1|0@socialism^1440004241#cu8g2nr:Moontouch$:Well this is a pretty underwhelming article, and I don't even endorse Sanders. The only actual attempt at argumentation in this monologue is Sander's opposition to normalizing trade relations with China and his concern over less and less commodities being produced in the US. The latter is an issue that is taken seriously by contemporary Marxists (Wolff off the top of my head) because workers don’t own the means of production under capitalism and have their labor outsourced to the Third World by the bourgeoisie. Opposing full trade relations with China would probably be done for any reason why leftists oppose free trade agreements, whether with China or NAFTA – they’re spearheaded by the bourgeois ruling class for their own interests. None of these two are right-wing or right-nationalist concerns.
3|0@socialism^1440007363#cu8ic3p:inthemouthsofmaggots$:I disagree. Protectionism is economic nationalism. Obviously I oppose free trade, but I don't think stirring the American masses into a xenophobic furor is the way to end false consciousness. Sanders should be addressing capitalism as the root problem, not outsourcing. Outsourcing is just a symptom. His decision to turn the rhetoric into "they're taking our jobs" is a distinctly right-wing talking point. Far from pursuing genuinely socialist politics, which is based on revolutionary internationalism, he opposes the international unity of the working class, calling instead for American workers to rally in defense of “their” national state against foreign capital. 
